WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.912
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:08.121
It's an incredibly exciting
time to have you here.

00:00:08.121 --> 00:00:10.801
There's so
much going on in the auto industry.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:13.690
And before we delve into
the trends within that,

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.970
and potentially your personal career,

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:21.070
and the leadership that you've shown, it'd
be great to look at the space as a whole.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:27.110
And it seems at the moment there's a sort
of existential threat where the need,

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.600
and indeed the desire to
own cars is coming down.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:37.000
How do you lead such a huge
iconic company in that context?

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.020
&gt;&gt; Well, I think, obviously we do a lot of
research to understand what's happening.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:45.090
We're definitely seeing people
are deciding to buy vehicles later.

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:46.951
Depending on where they live.

00:00:46.951 --> 00:00:50.608
And if you put the customer in
the center of how you look at this,

00:00:50.608 --> 00:00:54.265
it doesn't surprise me that,
if you're in New York City, and

00:00:54.265 --> 00:00:58.140
you pay a lot of money for a car, and
you pay a lot of money to park it.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.840
When you get it out to drive it,
you're in stop and go traffic.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.900
When you get to where you want to go,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.500
you're going to pay a lot of money
to park it if you can find a place.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.120
And so, you start to look at all
of those customer pain points.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.790
And you start to say, people
are still going to need to move from

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.870
point A to point B, but depending on if
they're in a dense urban environment,

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:18.240
what are they going to
need to travel with them?

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.000
Are they a single individual?

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.980
Do they have three children and
a Pack 'n Play?

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:28.190
So I think we try to look at and say,
let's really understand the customer.

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:31.060
And let's understand how we
can create value for them.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:34.770
And how we can provide new
experiences that are better for them.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:40.650
And that kind of drives us on how we make
decisions about what we invest in and

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.930
different concepts of why we did
the alliance with Lyft, why we bought

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:49.050
Cruise Automation, why we started
our own car sharing program, Maven.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.940
It's all with this intent to make sure
that we're looking and saying, how do we

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:57.030
provide the most value for the customer
as they get from point A to point B?

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.048
&gt;&gt; Right, and
in these times of sort of quick change,

00:02:00.048 --> 00:02:04.490
presumably that requires a change of
behavior equally at General Motors.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.780
And it's over 100 years old,
over 200,000 employees.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.214
I'm sure you know these stats.

00:02:13.214 --> 00:02:17.827
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But more seriously, historically,

00:02:17.827 --> 00:02:21.191
the focus has been on a long
product cycle, on lean operations,

00:02:21.191 --> 00:02:24.150
not on taking risks, on iterating quickly.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.600
As we move from a hardware centric
world to a more software focused one,

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:33.060
how do you change the behavior to become
more dynamic, more entrepreneurial?

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:37.099
&gt;&gt; Well, you make a really good point and
we've invested a lot of time in that.

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:39.858
People have this 100 year
old view of General Motors.

00:02:39.858 --> 00:02:43.035
And one of the stats that I throw
out that usually surprises people is

00:02:43.035 --> 00:02:45.822
almost 40% of the employees,
the salaried work force,

00:02:45.822 --> 00:02:48.682
has been with the company
less than five years.

00:02:48.682 --> 00:02:52.210
And so, there's a lot of new talent
that has come to the company.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:56.070
As we've done that, we've kind of mixed,
remixed the shift of.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.890
There used to be a lot of people
who worked on body structures, and

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.218
now we have a lot more software
engineers in the technical area, so

00:03:02.218 --> 00:03:08.560
we've really managed how we shift
the skill set of the organization.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.850
Because even if you take
the traditional car, ten years ago,

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:15.350
when you look at a car, it had a radio,
it maybe had some systems on it.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:19.237
But now, every vehicle we make,
even say the most base vehicle,

00:03:19.237 --> 00:03:21.792
has tens of millions of
lines of code in it.

00:03:21.792 --> 00:03:23.624
So we've been making that shift and

00:03:23.624 --> 00:03:26.760
have a lot of new talent that
has come to the company.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.260
But then the second part of your question
is, we're a long development cycle.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.160
If you start from a blank computer screen,

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.300
not a piece of paper, you're really
going to be on a four to five year

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.300
development plan to put a new
car into the marketplace.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:46.385
But when we're doing a new offering for
Maven, it's on a week cycle.

00:03:46.385 --> 00:03:50.623
It's how quickly do you create the
software to add the new functionality to

00:03:50.623 --> 00:03:51.167
the app?

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:55.539
And so,
we've really had to challenge ourself to

00:03:55.539 --> 00:04:00.535
know when we need to move at
a vehicle development cycle,

00:04:00.535 --> 00:04:05.140
because the average of
a car is over 11.4 years.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:10.089
So we're making a product that's going to
be on the road for a very long time,

00:04:10.089 --> 00:04:13.322
and safety is an overriding
priority with that.

00:04:13.322 --> 00:04:16.809
But yet, in different aspects
of our business now, what is

00:04:16.809 --> 00:04:21.430
the minimum viable product to put forward
that will really start to add value?

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.120
So we have to be working,
we talk about working at both speeds.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.974
One of the great things was
acquiring Cruise Automation.

00:04:28.974 --> 00:04:33.825
Because what we've done with Cruise,
and we've now given them responsibility

00:04:33.825 --> 00:04:38.605
not only to develop the artificial
intelligence, or the specific software for

00:04:38.605 --> 00:04:42.976
autonomists linked into the major
technical resources of the company.

00:04:42.976 --> 00:04:47.107
But we've also given them the
responsibility for commercialization and

00:04:47.107 --> 00:04:51.364
we have a, I'll say a very thin line of
a handful of people that they go to when

00:04:51.364 --> 00:04:54.716
they want to leverage the vast
resources of General Motors.

00:04:54.716 --> 00:04:58.978
There's like a pretty hard wall with the
big company wanting to go into the startup

00:04:58.978 --> 00:05:03.235
because we don't want to overwhelm
them with help and slow them down.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:06.675
And so,
it's really allowed us to kind of learn,

00:05:06.675 --> 00:05:12.040
I'll say at startup speed,
how to run a major initiative.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.864
A very important program that we're
working on from an autonomous driving

00:05:15.864 --> 00:05:16.714
perspective and

00:05:16.714 --> 00:05:21.038
we're able to start to infuse that type of
thinking into the broader organization.

00:05:21.038 --> 00:05:26.509
So the added benefit of acquiring Cruise
was not only the wonderful technology and

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:28.253
the talented people, but

00:05:28.253 --> 00:05:32.782
it was also just driving the whole
company to work at start up speed.

00:05:32.782 --> 00:05:35.078
&gt;&gt; And speaking about autonomous cars,

00:05:35.078 --> 00:05:38.670
I think we all acknowledge now
it's a matter of when not if.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.490
What should we as, sort of, citizens be
most excited about in that transformation?

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.022
What should we be most concerned about?

00:05:47.022 --> 00:05:50.493
&gt;&gt; I think we should be most
excited is safety, because 96%,

00:05:50.493 --> 00:05:55.298
if you look at the latest statistics, 96%
of all fatalities, and this is US data,

00:05:55.298 --> 00:05:58.400
because it's readily available,
are due to human error.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.550
And so,
as you get into autonomous vehicles,

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:07.677
you are going to be moving to a much safer
way to be moving from point A to point B.

00:06:07.677 --> 00:06:09.034
So I think that's one of
the most significance.

00:06:09.034 --> 00:06:14.459
And also, if done well,
is more convenient, gives you time back.

00:06:14.459 --> 00:06:18.998
You can be doing other things in
the vehicle, having different experiences.

00:06:18.998 --> 00:06:20.510
We call it a vehicle, but

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:24.187
whatever we want to call what
moves you from point A to point B.

00:06:24.187 --> 00:06:29.625
I think worried about is, I don't know
if the customers necessarily were.

00:06:29.625 --> 00:06:33.515
But I think it's also, I wouldn't call
it a worry as much as an understanding.

00:06:33.515 --> 00:06:37.245
Is that, for a very long time, autonomous
vehicles are going to be interacting with

00:06:38.505 --> 00:06:41.145
vehicles driven by humans.

00:06:41.145 --> 00:06:44.680
And it's going to be moving
to a much safer place,

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.930
but that doesn't mean that
everything is going to be perfect.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:48.823
Nothing in life is perfect.

00:06:48.823 --> 00:06:51.509
No software program is infinitely perfect.

00:06:51.509 --> 00:06:55.463
And so I think understanding how we
make that transition in today's world,

00:06:55.463 --> 00:06:59.478
I think will be important, that we spend
some time educating but also helping

00:06:59.478 --> 00:07:03.710
people to understand what the technology
is capable of and what it's not.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:07.076
And especially where we're going to be,
for a very long time,

00:07:07.076 --> 00:07:08.930
we'll be in a world that has both.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:14.185
&gt;&gt; And you mentioned the significant
investment you made in Lyft earlier.

00:07:14.185 --> 00:07:16.850
I think at one point there was even
talk about potentially acquiring it.

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:21.732
But what's the game plan there and
particularly give them what's happened

00:07:21.732 --> 00:07:25.478
more recently, how do you think
of trying to lead to Uber?

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:26.054
&gt;&gt; Well.

00:07:26.054 --> 00:07:29.848
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's lift

00:07:29.848 --> 00:07:33.331
subjective I would imagine.

00:07:33.331 --> 00:07:37.721
The way we look at it and why we did
the alliance is we believe that autonomous

00:07:37.721 --> 00:07:42.320
driving for end consumer will first
happen in a shared environment.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.357
Now, why is that?

00:07:43.357 --> 00:07:47.387
A one is because as the vehicles,
you first put the vehicle out,

00:07:47.387 --> 00:07:52.088
A, we want to own it until we go through
the rapid cycles of getting the sensor

00:07:52.088 --> 00:07:54.643
across town, there's going to be costs.

00:07:54.643 --> 00:07:58.040
So it's going to make sense to have
it in a A shared environment but

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:02.000
also because the vehicle will
start with limited capability.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.253
Right now we're limited on how fast the
vehicle could go based on how fast we can

00:08:05.253 --> 00:08:07.336
process the date that
coming in from the sensors.

00:08:07.336 --> 00:08:09.923
You know that will change and
that will change rapidly.

00:08:09.923 --> 00:08:15.980
So what will happen, the cost curve will
go down as the capability curve goes up.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.720
How does it manage a tunnel?

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:23.458
How does it manage different snow and fog,
and all the other different elements?

00:08:23.458 --> 00:08:28.366
And so, I think, that as we look at it,
that is why it was so

00:08:28.366 --> 00:08:31.095
important to have an alliance.

00:08:31.095 --> 00:08:35.518
We've actually learned a lot about the
ride sharing space through our alliance.

00:08:35.518 --> 00:08:39.610
But very important because that's where we
think the first major opportunity is for

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:41.610
autonomous vehicles.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:45.467
&gt;&gt; So maybe summing up all these
kind of first few questions,

00:08:45.467 --> 00:08:50.516
what does General Motors look like
in ten years' time, 20 years' time?

00:08:50.516 --> 00:08:52.220
&gt;&gt; I don't think anyone knows.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:57.015
But I think my view, and
what I work hard every day to make sure,

00:08:57.015 --> 00:09:01.720
is that General Motors is
leading the transformation.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.296
Because when I look at
the assets that we have.

00:09:03.296 --> 00:09:08.645
Not only the deep technical talent
of doing, again, machines and

00:09:08.645 --> 00:09:14.660
technology platforms that are on
the roads for more than a decade.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:18.692
And leading in how do we focus on the
customer to make sure that it's not just

00:09:18.692 --> 00:09:20.232
technology because we can.

00:09:20.232 --> 00:09:22.590
But how do we solve real issues?

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:27.890
How do we make the world better
from a customer perspective,

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:32.010
from an environmental perspective,
from a congestion perspective?

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:35.883
And so I believe that all the assets we
have that we have a unique ability and

00:09:35.883 --> 00:09:38.393
responsibility to live
that transformation.

00:09:38.393 --> 00:09:44.453
To do that exceptionally well and
improve the whole transportation space.

00:09:44.453 --> 00:09:49.790
&gt;&gt; Okay, the political environment
has also seen significant changes.

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:55.170
A big part of President Trump's campaign
was bringing jobs back to the US.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:59.560
General Motors has manufacturing
hubs all over the world.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:02.397
You're also part of his business council.

00:10:02.397 --> 00:10:04.705
How do you sort of balance that and

00:10:04.705 --> 00:10:09.660
how do you think about bringing all
manufacturing jobs back to the US?

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:14.650
&gt;&gt; Well, I think first, our general
philosophy is we build where we sell.

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:16.355
And in the United States, for example,

00:10:16.355 --> 00:10:20.870
70% of the vehicles we sell in the United
States are built in the United States.

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:24.670
If we look at just total number of
vehicles built here versus sold here

00:10:24.670 --> 00:10:28.580
it approaches 80%, so
we do have a lot of jobs.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:32.330
We have over 100,000 jobs
in this country alone.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:36.220
So, part of it was making sure
people understood our industry.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.820
My responsibility is to
manage the company and

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:44.150
deploy capital in the best interest
of our owners, our shareholders.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:46.806
And so I've got to look at that and
make sure.

00:10:46.806 --> 00:10:49.827
It's easy in the world to say,
I'm going to build a factory here and

00:10:49.827 --> 00:10:51.480
I'm going to bring a bunch of jobs here.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:55.742
But if that's not sustainable, if that
wasn't the best decision for the company,

00:10:55.742 --> 00:10:57.553
it's a very temporary situation.

00:10:57.553 --> 00:11:03.298
You have to have a strong, vibrant company
to provide jobs to have job security and

00:11:03.298 --> 00:11:05.645
provide jobs over the long haul.

00:11:05.645 --> 00:11:07.186
And I think that's what the goal is here.

00:11:07.186 --> 00:11:14.340
So I think we work on, how do we make
manufacturing more competitive here?

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:18.870
How do we take away,
across the globe there isn't necessarily

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:24.760
a level playing field and
what can come in versus can be exported.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.370
So there's a lot that we can work
on that's going to improve jobs.

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:32.078
But the foundation of it has to
be good business economic sense.

00:11:32.078 --> 00:11:35.698
Otherwise it's not sustainable and

00:11:35.698 --> 00:11:39.906
in the long term it's not good for anyone.

00:11:39.906 --> 00:11:44.254
&gt;&gt; Then sort looking specifically,
I have one of President Trump's tweets.

00:11:44.254 --> 00:11:48.558
It said General Motors is
sending Mexican made model of

00:11:48.558 --> 00:11:53.062
Chevy Cruze to US car dealers
tax free across the border.

00:11:53.062 --> 00:11:56.042
Making US [INAUDIBLE] pay big border tax.

00:11:56.042 --> 00:11:57.271
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; I recall that too.

00:11:57.271 --> 00:11:59.031
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:11:59.031 --> 00:12:00.804
they never sound as good
when you read them out.

00:12:00.804 --> 00:12:04.880
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:06.750
&gt;&gt; General Motors is clearly not alone

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:10.132
in facing tweets from President Trump.

00:12:10.132 --> 00:12:14.906
Given particularly the lack of clearly
defined policy that we've seen,

00:12:14.906 --> 00:12:17.295
how do you react to a tweet like that?

00:12:17.295 --> 00:12:19.900
&gt;&gt; Well, I think the first thing
is don't over train at it.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:25.020
Because if you step back and say, okay,
his message is jobs, that's pretty clear.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:28.960
We were within, I don't think it was
an hour before that tweet came out.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:32.730
We were able to put the facts on that
specific vehicle that yes it's built in

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:36.960
Mexico but, then it becomes
the economics of building cars.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:38.982
We probably sell, I don't know,

00:12:38.982 --> 00:12:42.761
less than 10,000 of those
vehicles in the United States.

00:12:42.761 --> 00:12:47.235
Because a hatch,
it's a segment that people want, but

00:12:47.235 --> 00:12:50.450
it's not a dominant segment.

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:51.972
And that's true for most countries.

00:12:51.972 --> 00:12:54.620
So, placing that business
in Mexico with all the free

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:57.300
trade agreements they have allows
you to offer that vehicle.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:01.720
If you said, I can only offer it if I
build it here, I wouldn't capitalize it.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:03.530
So, it wouldn't be available.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:05.250
And so
part of it was just explaining that,

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:10.390
explaining that the Cruze sedan
is built in Lordstown, Michigan.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:12.631
And a factory is dedicated to it.

00:13:12.631 --> 00:13:14.290
And I think that pretty much subdued that.

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:17.180
But I stepped back and said, okay I'm
not going to over train on the tweet.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:20.375
I'm certainly not going to
get into a tweeting exchange.

00:13:20.375 --> 00:13:22.742
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Let's put the facts.

00:13:22.742 --> 00:13:24.250
&gt;&gt; I don't have those tweets that you-
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] Yeah, I do.

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:29.190
But if you think about it,
I step back and said, okay.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:30.730
What is the real message?

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:35.390
And let's now work constructively
on trying to create more jobs.

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:39.119
One of the things I'm really proud about
at General Motors is with the 100,000

00:13:39.119 --> 00:13:39.961
people we employ.

00:13:39.961 --> 00:13:45.170
Those are good middle class or
better jobs in this country.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.625
And I'm really proud of
the jobs that we provide.

00:13:47.625 --> 00:13:50.280
So let's look at how does it
make good business sense for

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:53.580
us to do more of that as opposed
to responding to a tweet.?

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:59.827
&gt;&gt; Got it and I guess then more formally
lots of potential trade policy coming up.

00:13:59.827 --> 00:14:02.466
Is there anything that you're thinking

00:14:02.466 --> 00:14:07.210
will benefit General Motors substantially
or workers in the rust belt?

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.100
&gt;&gt; I think workers across the country,

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:14.700
I think getting a level plain field,
I think that's how we can really ask.

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:18.409
We shouldn't ask for
an advantage that is artificial.

00:14:18.409 --> 00:14:22.465
Again, my opinion,
let's have an equal playing field and

00:14:22.465 --> 00:14:25.962
then I can create more jobs
if I sell more vehicles.

00:14:25.962 --> 00:14:28.695
If I make more vehicles
here that I can export.

00:14:28.695 --> 00:14:33.700
Because it's not a 10, 20, or 30% tax
as they're sent to another country.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:37.275
I mean,
our main message is a level playing field,

00:14:37.275 --> 00:14:40.528
and then let us compete,
and we'll bet on us.

00:14:40.528 --> 00:14:45.673
&gt;&gt; Got it, and then we saw that some CEOs,
like Travis from Uber.

00:14:45.673 --> 00:14:49.317
Obviously chose not to be part of that
business council in the end after

00:14:49.317 --> 00:14:51.060
a reaction from their user base.

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:54.060
Was that something that
crossed your mind as well,

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:58.810
given with parts of your customers,
it's an unpopular administration?

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:03.391
&gt;&gt; First of all, it's our administration.

00:15:03.391 --> 00:15:08.319
I have voted in many elections, and there
have been times the candidate I voted for

00:15:08.319 --> 00:15:09.185
was elected.

00:15:09.185 --> 00:15:11.848
And there have been times that
candidate hasn't been, but

00:15:11.848 --> 00:15:13.081
it then is our government.

00:15:13.081 --> 00:15:15.797
And so when you're asked to serve and

00:15:15.797 --> 00:15:22.130
with as important as our industry is
to the country and jobs and what we do.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:25.581
To not take the seat at the table
if it's offered to you,

00:15:25.581 --> 00:15:28.086
I just think it is a missed opportunity.

00:15:28.086 --> 00:15:31.873
And so when I represent the company and
to have that voice,

00:15:31.873 --> 00:15:35.520
into the government, I think,
is vitally important.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.812
And I think it's proven
to be very important for

00:15:37.812 --> 00:15:40.112
us to communicate a very
complex supply chain.

00:15:40.112 --> 00:15:44.220
As it related to the border tax initially,
when those conversations.

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:47.699
Or the fact that there wasn't a level
playing field across many countries.

00:15:47.699 --> 00:15:51.150
That can send vehicles here with no tax or

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:55.016
to talk about job creation and
job training.

00:15:55.016 --> 00:15:59.371
There has just been a whole host of issues
that we have been able to have a strong

00:15:59.371 --> 00:16:04.409
voice into it So to miss that opportunity,
I wouldn't consider that.

00:16:04.409 --> 00:16:07.865
&gt;&gt; And you mentioned that
having a seat at the table,

00:16:07.865 --> 00:16:11.993
would you ever consider a more
permanent seat at the table?

00:16:11.993 --> 00:16:12.955
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think I just

00:16:12.955 --> 00:16:15.624
answered that question about a week and
a half ago.

00:16:15.624 --> 00:16:19.019
But I've been at General Motors for
37 years.

00:16:19.019 --> 00:16:19.821
There is just so

00:16:19.821 --> 00:16:23.667
many exciting things we're working
on with transforming the industry.

00:16:23.667 --> 00:16:27.980
That's where my heart is, that's where my
passion is and that's where I will work.

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:32.168
And I think I can contribute,
if we execute what we're working on,

00:16:32.168 --> 00:16:36.441
I think that we can really contribute
to the country and to the globe.

00:16:36.441 --> 00:16:41.349
because we do business in many countries,
so I think that's where I belong.

00:16:41.349 --> 00:16:42.483
&gt;&gt; Makes sense.

00:16:42.483 --> 00:16:44.634
I'll change my track.

00:16:44.634 --> 00:16:49.368
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So one of the other things is you,

00:16:49.368 --> 00:16:56.338
obviously, why are you considered one of
the faces of successful women in business.

00:16:56.338 --> 00:17:00.927
There sometimes appears to be
an expectation that will make you

00:17:00.927 --> 00:17:03.707
a champion of women in the workplace.

00:17:03.707 --> 00:17:08.417
Is that ever something that's
frustrating or how do you handle that?

00:17:08.417 --> 00:17:12.389
&gt;&gt; Well, I guess I'd like to be
a champion of people in the workplace.

00:17:12.389 --> 00:17:17.737
because I believe, fundamentally that
you'll have create the best experience,

00:17:17.737 --> 00:17:22.771
have the best results when you engage
the hearts and minds of your people.

00:17:22.771 --> 00:17:25.390
So that's the way I look at it.

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:30.181
Clearly, if I can be a role model for
young women to want to pursue a career in

00:17:30.181 --> 00:17:33.159
STEM, which I think is so
vitally important.

00:17:33.159 --> 00:17:36.629
Or for them to say I'm going to aspire
to do this because I've seen her.

00:17:36.629 --> 00:17:38.166
And she did it, so why can't I?

00:17:38.166 --> 00:17:44.056
And if I can provide that role model,
that makes me extremely happy.

00:17:44.056 --> 00:17:47.302
In my first year on the job,
I had somebody come up to me and said,

00:17:47.302 --> 00:17:50.510
Because of you, my daughter has
chosen to study engineering.

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:53.221
And it was like one of the best
comments you could get.

00:17:53.221 --> 00:17:56.750
And so, for sure that is very meaningful.

00:17:56.750 --> 00:18:00.431
At General Motors,
because you can't say diversity is so

00:18:00.431 --> 00:18:03.672
vitally important for
the success of our company.

00:18:03.672 --> 00:18:06.174
And it's not something that you can say,
well, I did it and it's done,

00:18:06.174 --> 00:18:07.829
it's something that you
continually work on.

00:18:07.829 --> 00:18:13.350
I champion a group of the most senior
women, so the top 35 women in the company.

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.864
But it's really for me, it's getting
them to say, okay, don't come to me and

00:18:16.864 --> 00:18:18.447
tell me everything that's wrong.

00:18:18.447 --> 00:18:19.890
You're a senior person in this company.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:21.949
Go tell me how you're going to
solve those problems, and

00:18:21.949 --> 00:18:23.577
what you're going to do to make it better.

00:18:23.577 --> 00:18:26.861
And we now meet three to
four times a year, and

00:18:26.861 --> 00:18:31.280
work on what are some of the issues
with women and diversity.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:34.074
because what I found earlier in my
career as I worked on diversity,

00:18:34.074 --> 00:18:37.084
is generally a lot of times when
you solve these issues from women,

00:18:37.084 --> 00:18:39.280
you solve these issues for everyone.

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:43.920
Because a lot of time men have the same
issues, or if it's a minority issue,

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:47.907
or whatever it is a lot of it can
get solved with solving the women,

00:18:47.907 --> 00:18:50.759
the issue that's termed the woman's issue.

00:18:50.759 --> 00:18:53.427
And so I'm dedicated to that.

00:18:53.427 --> 00:18:59.047
But I also believe it's you want to win
the hearts and minds of your entire team.

00:18:59.047 --> 00:19:05.778
&gt;&gt; Right, and if we now look back to
sort of the start of your career.

00:19:05.778 --> 00:19:09.713
You came through as a co-op
student with General Motors, and

00:19:09.713 --> 00:19:12.560
spent 37 years rising to the top.

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:13.854
A lot of us now,

00:19:13.854 --> 00:19:19.541
I think don't expect to have such
a long term career at one company.

00:19:19.541 --> 00:19:24.253
What would be your advice for
us in considering that, and

00:19:24.253 --> 00:19:27.210
if you're reviewing resumes now?

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:32.309
How'd you think about someone that's maybe
gotta wider breadth of knowledge versus

00:19:32.309 --> 00:19:36.936
a depth of knowledge, and someone that
stayed in one place for a longer time?

00:19:36.936 --> 00:19:40.257
&gt;&gt; Well, you meet people who have breadth
and depth, depending on what the role is.

00:19:40.257 --> 00:19:41.166
So I look for that.

00:19:41.166 --> 00:19:45.848
I don't necessarily look and say,
this person's been at four companies.

00:19:45.848 --> 00:19:47.657
And this person's been at one.

00:19:47.657 --> 00:19:49.671
I want to sometimes understand,

00:19:49.671 --> 00:19:54.079
because if the person's been at
four companies, why did they leave?

00:19:54.079 --> 00:19:56.508
And a lot of the times they leave for
very good reasons.

00:19:56.508 --> 00:20:00.026
Sometimes if you get to a point where,
well I had an issue here, then I had

00:20:00.026 --> 00:20:03.969
the same issue here, and I had the same
issue here, and I had the same issue here.

00:20:03.969 --> 00:20:05.913
Maybe you're the issue.

00:20:05.913 --> 00:20:06.589
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:20:06.589 --> 00:20:09.648
so, I kind of want to probe
a little on that, but

00:20:09.648 --> 00:20:12.638
beyond that, that's not important to me.

00:20:12.638 --> 00:20:14.562
It's really what the skills,
and abilities,

00:20:14.562 --> 00:20:17.460
and experiences that they bring,
and the leadership that they bring.

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:22.931
I've been at General Motors for 37 years,
and I wouldn't say when I started and

00:20:22.931 --> 00:20:25.303
graduated as a co-op student
that I was going to be here.

00:20:25.303 --> 00:20:29.001
I didnt have this plan, well I will
be at General Motors for 37 years.

00:20:29.001 --> 00:20:31.367
I kept having great
experiences put in front of me.

00:20:31.367 --> 00:20:32.363
They sent me here.

00:20:32.363 --> 00:20:35.548
We were just talking in the back that,
when I first came back from the business

00:20:35.548 --> 00:20:38.896
school, there had been a lot of change in
turmoil and I didn't have the best job.

00:20:38.896 --> 00:20:42.424
And it wasn't one of those
that I was passionate about.

00:20:42.424 --> 00:20:46.427
And fortunately in about
a year's period of time, and

00:20:46.427 --> 00:20:51.051
especially by two,
the situation changed and I chose to stay.

00:20:51.051 --> 00:20:52.775
So I always believe in your career.

00:20:52.775 --> 00:20:58.000
Life's too short to work for a company or
be in a position that you don't love.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.478
Now, work is work,
I had this funny text from my nephew,

00:21:01.478 --> 00:21:05.920
who's now graduating from a BBA in
the business school at Michigan.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:08.118
And it was his first job,
and he texted me.

00:21:08.118 --> 00:21:12.180
I'm sitting at a computer and I've got to
do all this work on spreadsheets and I,

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:14.351
it was kind of like, this isn't much fun.

00:21:14.351 --> 00:21:17.665
I said, yeah that's why they call it work.

00:21:17.665 --> 00:21:19.720
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You're going to have to work and

00:21:19.720 --> 00:21:23.100
not every days,
you can't measure your career on everyday.

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:26.586
But if you find yourself after
six months after a year that,

00:21:26.586 --> 00:21:28.128
work is not challenging.

00:21:28.128 --> 00:21:31.170
Or you don't enjoy it, or
you're not energized by it,

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:33.774
you should probably find
something else to do.

00:21:33.774 --> 00:21:35.634
Again because life's too short.

00:21:35.634 --> 00:21:39.747
I am fortunate after 37 years, and again
it wasn't always just a straight line,

00:21:39.747 --> 00:21:41.702
there were ups and downs in that curve.

00:21:41.702 --> 00:21:45.545
But I've been offered tremendous
opportunities which you can have at a big

00:21:45.545 --> 00:21:47.991
company, like a company
like General Motors.

00:21:47.991 --> 00:21:53.076
To have worked in communications,
and HR, and run an assembly plant,

00:21:53.076 --> 00:21:59.291
and led the machinery equipment, purchase
for the globe, and product development.

00:21:59.291 --> 00:22:00.960
I feel like I've had a bunch of careers,

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:03.424
they've just all happened to
have been at General Motors.

00:22:05.782 --> 00:22:11.160
&gt;&gt; So having had two years at the GSB, and
to have enjoyed the life in California,

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:17.111
the transition back to Detroit, was that-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:22:17.111 --> 00:22:18.040
&gt;&gt; Was that an easy one?

00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:20.494
And I guess as graduates again here,

00:22:20.494 --> 00:22:25.653
Detroit is probably not one of the places
that a lot of us are looking for work.

00:22:25.653 --> 00:22:28.379
How'd you think about that now?

00:22:28.379 --> 00:22:31.247
What are the positives we're
missing about Detroit?

00:22:31.247 --> 00:22:34.975
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, there are so many.

00:22:34.975 --> 00:22:37.581
First of all, it was easy for me to go
back home because that's where my husband

00:22:37.581 --> 00:22:38.983
was, and that's where my family was.

00:22:38.983 --> 00:22:41.851
And I'm a pretty family-oriented person.

00:22:41.851 --> 00:22:45.001
So if General Motors had said, hey you
could stay here, I probably had said,

00:22:45.001 --> 00:22:47.340
well unless my husband's coming
here I want to go there.

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:50.379
But again, family oriented person
at the time, my parents were there.

00:22:50.379 --> 00:22:52.272
And so, that wasn't that hard a decision.

00:22:52.272 --> 00:22:57.484
Now I have to admit one of the reasons
I love being on the Stanford advisory

00:22:57.484 --> 00:23:02.712
board for the GSB, as well as the board
of trustees, I love the Bay Area.

00:23:02.712 --> 00:23:07.130
And, one of the things that I, and
Glen will remember this when we designed.

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:11.252
We actually do a custom program for
our top leadership.

00:23:11.252 --> 00:23:15.141
And one of our third,
I think completing our third cohort now.

00:23:15.141 --> 00:23:18.719
It's a program that runs a year
with four different weeks.

00:23:18.719 --> 00:23:23.626
And when I first came to the school I
said Silicon Valley doesn't have the, or

00:23:23.626 --> 00:23:27.721
Palo Alto, you don't have the lock
on innovation creativity.

00:23:27.721 --> 00:23:32.250
And I want to infuse that back into
the company actually, across the globe.

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:36.034
And that was kind of the start of
designing the program that we did around

00:23:36.034 --> 00:23:40.767
design thinking, and leadership, and it's
actually one of the most popular classes,

00:23:40.767 --> 00:23:44.829
it's not a class it's a development
program, it's probably of all time.

00:23:44.829 --> 00:23:48.698
So it's something that's worked
out exceptionally well, so

00:23:48.698 --> 00:23:52.206
it's a beautiful climate
there's a lot of energy here.

00:23:52.206 --> 00:23:56.275
But Detroit is different,
Detroit you have seasons you have.

00:23:56.275 --> 00:23:59.205
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Come on.

00:23:59.205 --> 00:24:04.260
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But and my mother

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:07.950
used to say that too, but I do say just to
be telling you this I say ever and since I

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:11.300
spent two years out here my blood thinned
cause I'm always cold when its winter.

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:12.842
If its below 60 its cold.

00:24:12.842 --> 00:24:16.392
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; However in saying that I do enjoy it is

00:24:16.392 --> 00:24:18.040
a different climate.

00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:22.020
And the midwest is a different
type of community.

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:27.191
And that's where I've grown up so
that's home for me, and so I do enjoy it.

00:24:27.191 --> 00:24:31.501
Again, what I think is so
interesting about Detroit right now, and

00:24:31.501 --> 00:24:35.735
again if you don't like the Colts,
you're not going to be happy,

00:24:35.735 --> 00:24:38.324
I'm not going to try to put a bow on that.

00:24:38.324 --> 00:24:42.745
But what is really exciting right now to
be in Detroit, is because the city is

00:24:42.745 --> 00:24:47.600
going through just a complete renaissance,
and it's fun to be a part of that.

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:51.050
It's fun to see the new
restaurants start up, and

00:24:51.050 --> 00:24:55.750
the new cultural things that you can do.

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:58.620
And just to be a part of how
we're reinvigorating the city,

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:00.310
the entrepreneurship.

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:02.820
People coming to the town to
watch the new buildings come up.

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:06.780
You know, I joked about the fact that they
built a Whole Foods in downtown Detroit,

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:08.910
which five years ago people
would've been like, no.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:11.550
But they built a Whole Foods and

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:15.040
the biggest problem with the Whole Foods
is the parking lot's not big enough.

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:19.550
Because, when you look at all the young
professionals that have come into

00:25:19.550 --> 00:25:22.480
the city, when they work all day,
it's part of their routine.

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:27.360
So, it's really fun to be a part of a city
that's going through this rebirth and

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:29.190
to be a part of it.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:33.380
And the other thing, why we're
drawing a lot of entrepreneurs there

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:34.930
is because it's pretty
cheap to live there.

00:25:34.930 --> 00:25:37.720
So you can start a new business,
you can launch something.

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:41.853
So, I love it here but I love it there.

00:25:41.853 --> 00:25:43.230
&gt;&gt; That makes sense.

00:25:43.230 --> 00:25:48.280
And if we now go,
just ahead in your career,

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:53.210
you became CEO, there were other
internal candidates in the running.

00:25:53.210 --> 00:25:57.980
They changed from peers to becoming
people that reported in to you.

00:25:57.980 --> 00:26:00.160
How did you handle that transition?

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:05.660
&gt;&gt; Well, I think first of all,
we had a strong relationship before.

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:10.820
We had worked together before, and I have
a very collaborative leadership style,

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:14.470
so I want to get the best
views on the table.

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:18.120
And I think, because we knew each other so
well, it wasn't like I said something and

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:19.460
then everybody froze.

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:20.900
And I want people to challenge me.

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:23.460
I'm going to, you know, come at an issue,
and if I've got a point of view I'm going

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:26.320
to come at it pretty hard and
be pretty convicted.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:31.530
But, if someone presents a compelling
counterpoint, I want to hear that.

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:35.190
And so I try to make sure there's
a lot of constructive tension, and

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:36.530
that all views are heard.

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:39.400
There's times on really important
decisions where I go to every single

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:43.470
person around the table and
say, what do you think?

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:47.450
And I've also done work with my senior
leadership team to build trust.

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:50.780
We actually do off sites
two to three times a year.

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:52.400
Well we go in to make sure,

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:56.440
we know how to give each other
constructive negative feedback and

00:26:56.440 --> 00:27:00.680
have a direct conversation why I don't
agree with you and it's not personal.

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:04.780
So I think because that's my style
of being pretty collaborative,

00:27:04.780 --> 00:27:07.340
we were able to work
through it pretty quickly.

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:11.297
There was a probably a couple
little where you kind of had

00:27:11.297 --> 00:27:15.685
a re-negotiate how the relationship
was going to work but,

00:27:15.685 --> 00:27:20.520
I would say a weeks, maybe a few
months in it was kind of a nonissue.

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:25.288
&gt;&gt; That's a collaborative style is that
partly because of the experience you had,

00:27:25.288 --> 00:27:29.142
the GSP as well the offsite you have
talked about sound like some of

00:27:29.142 --> 00:27:30.840
the courses that we do here.

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:35.450
Was that like big part of
the collaborative style that you used?

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:38.120
&gt;&gt; I think it's a good question,
I mean I learned so much here.

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:40.980
I always say when people ask me,
what did you learn,

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:42.931
I didn't know what I didn't know.

00:27:42.931 --> 00:27:46.335
I had an engineering background,
I had worked in a big company,

00:27:46.335 --> 00:27:47.890
but more in a focused area.

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:50.830
And so, it was so
broadening to come here and understand

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:55.750
the several aspects of business, so
it's always been a foundation for me.

00:27:55.750 --> 00:28:00.420
But I think it probably does route back
to all those group projects you're doing

00:28:00.420 --> 00:28:04.330
now where you could probably write
the paper a little faster, but

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:08.580
it wouldn't be as good because it
wouldn't be fusing together four views or

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:10.930
having those debates about, what's better?

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:12.230
And I think you're right.

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:16.740
I think that it takes more time,
but in the end you get,

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:21.230
i think a much better answer that you can
then execute, because the other thing is,

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:25.570
once you go through that
process of debating,

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:28.350
everybody's on board, you know,
if you're doing it well,

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:30.930
you get everyone on board and
then you execute a lot faster.

00:28:33.820 --> 00:28:37.210
&gt;&gt; Then right at the time
you did become CEO you had

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:41.090
to immediately handle the faulty
ignition switch crisis.

00:28:41.090 --> 00:28:47.560
And how, if you had been CEO before that
rather than in the direct aftermath,

00:28:47.560 --> 00:28:49.929
was there anything you
could've done differently?

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:54.220
Or any changes that you
could of made to avoid it.

00:28:54.220 --> 00:28:54.724
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:28:54.724 --> 00:29:00.276
clearly if we'd known about it sooner
we've could of made better decisions.

00:29:00.276 --> 00:29:04.656
But one of the things that I think is
not well known from the way that it was

00:29:04.656 --> 00:29:07.649
covered in the media was
this was an issue that was

00:29:07.649 --> 00:29:11.980
unfortunate that over a long
period of time mistakes were made.

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:13.920
But it was the internal company.

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:17.700
And actually the team who finally
figured out what was happening and

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:21.070
brought the issue forward, and
we decided to do the recall so.

00:29:21.070 --> 00:29:25.180
But clearly there were a lot of mistakes
made along the way and, you know,

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:26.150
I don't know.

00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:28.900
The minute we found out about it,
we acted.

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:32.180
And the minute we knew,
we were guided by three principles,

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:35.140
of doing the right thing for
the customer, being transparent.

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:37.710
And doing everything in our power to
make sure it never happened again,

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:41.470
which meant changing a lot of
processes and the way work was done.

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:44.200
And so, I think it's one of those things,

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:46.340
you don't get to choose
when you have a crisis.

00:29:46.340 --> 00:29:51.320
When you have it, you have to deal with it
then, and you don't get to put it off and

00:29:51.320 --> 00:29:52.880
say I'll deal with it next month.

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:56.410
You deal with it now, because that's
just the way the world works.

00:29:56.410 --> 00:30:00.730
&gt;&gt; And throughout that time,
you made a significant effort to rebuild

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:06.950
General Motor's image with increase
number of public appearances, and so on.

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:11.220
How did you consider trying to
be proactive verses reactive in

00:30:11.220 --> 00:30:12.550
that time of crisis?

00:30:12.550 --> 00:30:16.390
&gt;&gt; I think you have to be proactive
because especially with how fast

00:30:16.390 --> 00:30:20.860
communications happen today
If you don't tell if you

00:30:20.860 --> 00:30:26.280
don't get the facts out on the table that
void will be filled with misinformation.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:30.102
And so we thought it was very important
that's why transparency was one of

00:30:30.102 --> 00:30:33.798
the kind of guiding principles we use
to make sure we will be [INAUDIBLE] at

00:30:33.798 --> 00:30:38.350
the same time, you can't answer
questions you don't know the answer to.

00:30:38.350 --> 00:30:41.250
And so, there were times where
I had to say, I don't know.

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:43.920
And, you know, I was criticized for
that when I went to Congress.

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:48.310
You know, even made fun of
on Saturday night live, but,

00:30:48.310 --> 00:30:50.876
that's a fun thing to wake up to.

00:30:50.876 --> 00:30:54.290
[LAUGH] And my brother calling me, Mary!

00:30:54.290 --> 00:30:55.010
Mary!

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:55.935
I'm like.
My gosh.

00:30:55.935 --> 00:31:01.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But, you don't really you have

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:07.190
to tell the story and you have to answer
the questions when you know the answer.

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:09.530
One of the things and
I encourage all of you, cause your so

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:14.080
tempted because you think
you know what happened.

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:17.900
Had I done that I would
have answered wrong.

00:31:17.900 --> 00:31:21.006
And I would've probably caused
a lot more harm to the company.

00:31:21.006 --> 00:31:24.510
So you want to get to the facts
as quickly as you can but

00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:27.843
if you don't have facts you can't,
it's this rationing,

00:31:27.843 --> 00:31:31.160
because you got to move as fast as you
can but you have to do it with facts.

00:31:31.160 --> 00:31:35.960
And for me the way I worked
on that was to have a cross

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:38.790
functional team that kind of had,
here's the engineering view, and

00:31:38.790 --> 00:31:41.980
the finance view, and the legal view,
and the communications view.

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:44.947
And the customer sales view,
let's all get in a room and

00:31:44.947 --> 00:31:49.428
let's figure out what are we all hearing,
what do we need to do, what are the facts,

00:31:49.428 --> 00:31:51.978
are you sure, okay,
now we're going to move.

00:31:51.978 --> 00:31:56.229
&gt;&gt; And now as you look back over your
career, one of the quotes that I saw

00:31:56.229 --> 00:32:01.480
as we were preparing for this was
that you've never asked for a raise.

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:05.069
And if you were now at the start
of that career journey,

00:32:05.069 --> 00:32:07.811
would you think about that differently?

00:32:07.811 --> 00:32:12.183
&gt;&gt; I guess the way I look at it, it
kind of gets back to what I said before.

00:32:12.183 --> 00:32:16.642
If you don't love your job, if you don't
think you're being fairly compensated, or

00:32:16.642 --> 00:32:20.900
challenged, or it's not a fulfilling job,
you need to act on that.

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:26.020
I think I found through my career,
pay wasn't one of the issues that I had.

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:30.807
I wasn't saying never do it, I'm just
saying I found myself in a position where

00:32:30.807 --> 00:32:34.889
there were times I had to ask for
say, hey, I know this is my job now.

00:32:34.889 --> 00:32:39.123
And I'm going to do this really well,
but here are where my interests are and

00:32:39.123 --> 00:32:43.372
I'd like to have the opportunity to
be considered for roles in this area.

00:32:43.372 --> 00:32:47.546
I think that's your obligation and
I did proactively advocate for

00:32:47.546 --> 00:32:50.370
myself in those types of situations.

00:32:50.370 --> 00:32:53.545
I just never had an issue where I thought
I wasn't being fairly compensated.

00:32:53.545 --> 00:33:00.100
&gt;&gt; Good, and final question from me
now before we open up to the audience.

00:33:00.100 --> 00:33:04.891
Also as part of the research, we read
about your sort of never happier than

00:33:04.891 --> 00:33:09.163
actually on the test track,
putting the cars through the paces.

00:33:09.163 --> 00:33:12.879
Is there anything else we would be
potentially surprised to know about?

00:33:12.879 --> 00:33:17.121
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Probably [LAUGH].

00:33:17.121 --> 00:33:20.809
But no just from a work perspective,
again, people say,

00:33:20.809 --> 00:33:23.960
why did you stay at General Motors so
long?

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:25.746
There is something about
the auto industry.

00:33:25.746 --> 00:33:28.277
And there's been a couple times
now where we've had people leave

00:33:28.277 --> 00:33:29.390
the industry and come back.

00:33:29.390 --> 00:33:32.568
And when I'm interviewing, I'll say,
so why do you want to come back?

00:33:32.568 --> 00:33:34.080
And they'll be, because I miss it.

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:39.727
because there's just an excitement about
building cars, or trucks, or crossovers.

00:33:39.727 --> 00:33:43.111
And people name them and
they write songs about them, and

00:33:43.111 --> 00:33:47.710
they pick it because this fits my
personality or this fits my functionality.

00:33:47.710 --> 00:33:51.780
But for a lot of people a car is kind
of an extension of themselves and

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:54.380
it's fun to be in that business.

00:33:54.380 --> 00:33:58.892
And then, I feel like we're
in the technology business,

00:33:58.892 --> 00:34:03.973
we're in the fashion business,
and so I just love the industry.

00:34:03.973 --> 00:34:06.588
And I would say my best days are either
spending time at design staff,

00:34:06.588 --> 00:34:09.037
because then you're looking at what
is the next generation of this?

00:34:09.037 --> 00:34:12.876
Or, what is the all new vehicle
in this segment going to be?

00:34:12.876 --> 00:34:17.220
The minute I see it,
I'm why is that two years out?

00:34:17.220 --> 00:34:18.532
I want it now.

00:34:18.532 --> 00:34:21.268
Or getting to spend time in
our Milford proving grounds or

00:34:21.268 --> 00:34:24.916
some of the test tracks we have around
the world, and drive in our vehicles.

00:34:24.916 --> 00:34:27.374
And when I was running
product development,

00:34:27.374 --> 00:34:30.680
every Friday afternoon we'd go
out to our proving grounds.

00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:33.312
And we'd drive our new cars, or
cars that were under development,

00:34:33.312 --> 00:34:34.940
we'd drive it against the competition.

00:34:34.940 --> 00:34:39.945
And we'd spend the afternoon and
it's what we do.

00:34:39.945 --> 00:34:41.779
So it's a lot of fun.

00:34:41.779 --> 00:34:45.893
&gt;&gt; Amazing, well,
this by now then look to my colleagues.

00:34:54.775 --> 00:34:58.380
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Maurine Gauge Capello,
and I'm a MBA two here.

00:34:58.380 --> 00:35:02.390
We talk a lot about culture
at Stanford and I wonder for

00:35:02.390 --> 00:35:07.270
you, you're kind of leading GM through
this merger of cultures of an automotive

00:35:07.270 --> 00:35:11.948
world that is kind of slower returns,
longer cycles, a lot of capital.

00:35:11.948 --> 00:35:16.456
Versus like this technology innovation,
cycle of high returns, quick innovation,

00:35:16.456 --> 00:35:20.097
and I'm curious how you think about
leading a company through that and

00:35:20.097 --> 00:35:22.153
merging those two cultures together?

00:35:22.153 --> 00:35:27.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah, one of the ways to do
that is to bring in talent.

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:32.788
We brought in a lot of talent who
understood the vehicle development cycle

00:35:32.788 --> 00:35:37.350
that you talked about,
the longer capital intensive cycle.

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:40.740
But really to do it quickly,
you had to bring people in.

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:44.604
And you had to get them in the room and
get them debating it and

00:35:44.604 --> 00:35:46.781
coming up with the right answer.

00:35:46.781 --> 00:35:50.950
Because you can do certain things
especially in the vehicle.

00:35:50.950 --> 00:35:54.910
When you look at all the things you can
do in a vehicle now with connecting

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:57.555
your phone and
all that different technology.

00:35:57.555 --> 00:36:01.981
Or just the different functionality better
word that you can do in a car now that you

00:36:01.981 --> 00:36:04.380
couldn't do five or ten years ago.

00:36:04.380 --> 00:36:07.536
But how that work speed,
how you're technically doing that and

00:36:07.536 --> 00:36:09.172
still protecting the braking and

00:36:09.172 --> 00:36:12.285
the steering system, but
providing all this functionality.

00:36:12.285 --> 00:36:15.120
And most people don't understand, they'll
be like, well, why can't I control this?

00:36:15.120 --> 00:36:18.957
Well, that's pretty core to
the engine performance type of thing.

00:36:18.957 --> 00:36:21.866
So, but
getting the two mindsets in the room and

00:36:21.866 --> 00:36:26.688
coming up with the winning solution is
the best way to do it fast, I've found.

00:36:26.688 --> 00:36:29.214
And then just from a culture perspective,

00:36:29.214 --> 00:36:32.239
to me when I look at culture
it's about behaviors.

00:36:32.239 --> 00:36:35.610
So when people ask me what
are you most worried about?

00:36:35.610 --> 00:36:38.689
I'm confident we have good answers but
will we get them to market fast enough?

00:36:38.689 --> 00:36:44.055
And so, constantly re-emphasizing the
speed, but yet you can't, you sometimes,

00:36:44.055 --> 00:36:48.762
an organization, especially an engineering
organization will take you so

00:36:48.762 --> 00:36:53.410
literally, that you have to make sure
that you're talking about safety.

00:36:53.410 --> 00:36:57.386
One of the things we always say, we want
results, but we want it with integrity.

00:36:57.386 --> 00:37:01.410
And so, it's really, to me,
talking a lot about behaviors.

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:06.137
But when you're trying to blend two very
different worlds, it's pretty hard to

00:37:06.137 --> 00:37:10.811
take this person and or this person and
switch them and have that be successful.

00:37:10.811 --> 00:37:13.642
But you put them together and
they can be really powerful.

00:37:20.779 --> 00:37:25.250
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] Jim Phelps, MBA 79.

00:37:25.250 --> 00:37:32.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE].

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:33.341
&gt;&gt; No cane, no walker.

00:37:33.341 --> 00:37:38.225
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Going back to the breadth

00:37:38.225 --> 00:37:43.082
versus depth conversation
that you had a while back.

00:37:43.082 --> 00:37:47.202
I wondered if you might be able
to comment on the increasing push

00:37:47.202 --> 00:37:50.857
at both the undergraduate and
graduate level to focus,

00:37:50.857 --> 00:37:53.910
be more capable the moment you come out.

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:56.730
Commit and pick majors earlier.

00:37:56.730 --> 00:38:02.600
In graduate school, get concentrations
that are more finely tuned.

00:38:02.600 --> 00:38:06.787
And contrast that with the recent
comments by Sam Palmisano,

00:38:06.787 --> 00:38:12.034
the recently retired chairman and
head of another technical company, IBM.

00:38:12.034 --> 00:38:16.114
[COUGH] That he says, I think we
have a need for more breadth for

00:38:16.114 --> 00:38:20.430
people that can communicate,
can be cross disciplinary.

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:25.890
How do you see that working
out at a firm like GM and what

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:31.319
can you say to the student body that's
facing these sorts of questions right now?

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:34.810
&gt;&gt; It's really interesting.

00:38:34.810 --> 00:38:39.570
Again, from a company perspective,
I need people who have tremendous depth.

00:38:39.570 --> 00:38:43.102
If you're a chief engineer of a vehicle,
that's generally,

00:38:43.102 --> 00:38:47.235
by the time you get to be a chief you've
been in that vehicle development,

00:38:47.235 --> 00:38:49.987
being on a product program for
about 15 years.

00:38:49.987 --> 00:38:53.952
Because the cycles of learning, really
being able to make all of the decisions to

00:38:53.952 --> 00:38:56.900
integrate every single
system in the vehicle.

00:38:56.900 --> 00:39:00.337
The way we do it, it's a really small
team that is the chief engineer, and

00:39:00.337 --> 00:39:03.108
then the electrical group, and
the power train group, and

00:39:03.108 --> 00:39:07.067
the body structures group, and the
infotainment, they're all coming together.

00:39:07.067 --> 00:39:09.929
But they're the ones that have
got to make those tradeoffs so.

00:39:09.929 --> 00:39:13.520
And there are certain areas where
we just need tremendous staff.

00:39:14.870 --> 00:39:19.070
And then there's areas where you need
somebody say, yeah, I understand this but

00:39:19.070 --> 00:39:21.140
I do need the breadth because
you need to make the trade offs.

00:39:21.140 --> 00:39:24.000
So it's kind of you need to make
the trade offs this way or this way.

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:26.030
That's why I say you need both.

00:39:26.030 --> 00:39:29.160
And I'm going to answer the second
part of it as a mom though.

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:33.750
So I have a sophomore in college and
a high school senior.

00:39:33.750 --> 00:39:36.930
And I just, we are just asking.

00:39:36.930 --> 00:39:39.527
I go back, and not that we can
go back to when I was a kid.

00:39:39.527 --> 00:39:42.701
But how can you know
when you're 16 years old,

00:39:42.701 --> 00:39:46.120
exactly what you want to do for
the rest of your life?

00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:51.510
I'm not sure I know what I want to do
at age 55, for the rest of my life.

00:39:52.700 --> 00:39:57.500
So I think there's a need to,
if you're studying engineering,

00:39:57.500 --> 00:40:01.400
to still have some broadening classes and
some,

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:06.517
from a From a liberal arts perspective,
or the liberal arts school,

00:40:06.517 --> 00:40:09.722
or to understand philosophy,
I think it makes you a better engineer.

00:40:09.722 --> 00:40:13.060
So this, I'm only going to do this,
and I'm going to focus, and

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:15.335
I'm going to be the best
person on this one technology.

00:40:16.935 --> 00:40:21.177
It's one thing if you make that
decision once you get into your career.

00:40:21.177 --> 00:40:24.640
I remember one of the most valued people
when I was running the manufacturing

00:40:24.640 --> 00:40:26.655
engineering group with my welding expert.

00:40:27.655 --> 00:40:31.405
And he had spent his career
becoming a welding expert.

00:40:31.405 --> 00:40:35.585
And grew into that role and that's the
kind of depth I'm talking about you need.

00:40:35.585 --> 00:40:38.621
But he probably made that decision
in his thirties or forties.

00:40:38.621 --> 00:40:43.296
Not at 16, 17, 18, when you're
picking an undergraduate major.

00:40:43.296 --> 00:40:48.090
So I'm a big proponent that
as you're at a university,

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:52.479
yes, if you know for
sure this is what you want to do.

00:40:52.479 --> 00:40:55.207
I still think you should get
some broadening classes and

00:40:55.207 --> 00:40:58.080
challenge yourself to think
a little differently.

00:40:58.080 --> 00:41:00.185
If you don't know, that's okay!

00:41:00.185 --> 00:41:01.900
And then you need to take a broad sweep.

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:05.700
So again, I have one child who knows
exactly what he wants to do, and

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:06.880
I have another who doesn't.

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:10.899
And I'm telling them both, take some of
those classes that are going to broaden.

00:41:10.899 --> 00:41:13.564
That you're not sure about because, again,

00:41:13.564 --> 00:41:16.377
I came here thinking I was
kind of checking a box.

00:41:16.377 --> 00:41:18.368
And I didn't know what I didn't know.

00:41:18.368 --> 00:41:23.142
So it scares me how we're pretty soon
you're going to be in middle school and

00:41:23.142 --> 00:41:24.996
have to declare a career so.

00:41:24.996 --> 00:41:29.728
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Christoph Meyer MBA two I'd

00:41:29.728 --> 00:41:31.516
love to hear your perspectives.

00:41:31.516 --> 00:41:36.270
So many companies have entered the
automotive space including tech companies.

00:41:36.270 --> 00:41:38.983
What competitor keeps you up at night?

00:41:38.983 --> 00:41:41.248
&gt;&gt; Well, I like to describe
ourself as a tech company.

00:41:41.248 --> 00:41:45.526
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Tens of millions of lines of code in

00:41:45.526 --> 00:41:46.916
every vehicle, but okay.

00:41:46.916 --> 00:41:52.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But to your point, they all scare me.

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:54.413
It's my job to be paranoid about that.

00:41:54.413 --> 00:41:59.244
I mean, there are some incredibly,
when you look at the big players in the,

00:41:59.244 --> 00:42:03.865
I'll say in the traditional sense,
the OEMs, they're very capable.

00:42:03.865 --> 00:42:07.094
Whether you're talking about Asian
competitors, or German competitors,

00:42:07.094 --> 00:42:10.210
Korean competitor, very capable,
so they keep me up at night.

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:14.503
And then when you look at the resources
that some of the Apples, and the Googles,

00:42:14.503 --> 00:42:16.411
and some of the startups can bring.

00:42:16.411 --> 00:42:18.646
I mean we bought a startup because, and

00:42:18.646 --> 00:42:22.179
we made that decision could we
have grown that talent inside?

00:42:22.179 --> 00:42:26.538
Probably, it was a heck of a lot faster
to take somebody who had been working on

00:42:26.538 --> 00:42:30.177
it a couple years and then marry
the two and find the right power.

00:42:30.177 --> 00:42:34.905
So whether it's the big tech companies,
or the traditional OEMs, or

00:42:34.905 --> 00:42:37.570
the startups, they all worry me.

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:42.530
And that's my job to
worry about all of that.

00:42:42.530 --> 00:42:48.601
&gt;&gt; Alex Minkey, MBA one, I was curious to
get your thoughts on the growth of EV's,

00:42:48.601 --> 00:42:50.670
head winds and tail winds.

00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:54.538
How fast your expecting them to grow and
GM strategy within that.

00:42:54.538 --> 00:42:57.067
&gt;&gt; So from a electric vehicle perspective,

00:42:57.067 --> 00:43:00.650
I think it's definitely part
of the future solution.

00:43:00.650 --> 00:43:04.020
When you look at the environmental
concerns, it's absolutely a must.

00:43:04.020 --> 00:43:06.308
As are fuel cells, and
we invest heavily in both.

00:43:06.308 --> 00:43:11.767
I think EVs will come first
kind of down the learning curve.

00:43:11.767 --> 00:43:16.640
Because we're still working on solving the
kind of the energy density issues around

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.666
batteries, and the cost issues.

00:43:18.666 --> 00:43:22.760
Although, we had tremendous learnings
from our first generation Volt,

00:43:22.760 --> 00:43:26.400
to our second generation Volt,
to now the Chevrolet Volt.

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:30.650
I keep seeing the costs come down and
the energy density go up.

00:43:30.650 --> 00:43:35.625
So I believe we're close, in fact, General
Motors we have put out externally I said

00:43:35.625 --> 00:43:38.427
we have challenged ourself
to be the first OEM or

00:43:38.427 --> 00:43:42.580
company that has profitable electric
vehicles that are affordable.

00:43:42.580 --> 00:43:45.410
Because we believe it's so
much a part of the future.

00:43:45.410 --> 00:43:50.040
The biggest volume is going to first be
in China because they're regulating it.

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:52.424
And when you look at the size
of the Chinese market,

00:43:52.424 --> 00:43:54.704
last year it was 28
million new vehicles sold.

00:43:54.704 --> 00:44:00.357
Whether it's 18, 19, or 20,
10% of that market by mandate.

00:44:00.357 --> 00:44:06.820
Well, 10, 12, 14% will be electric or
either fully ED or a plug in hybrid.

00:44:06.820 --> 00:44:13.470
So definitely moving here, there's
a part that's an emotional decision and

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:17.800
then for the bulk of people who buy
vehicles, there's a financial decision.

00:44:17.800 --> 00:44:21.923
And with low gas prices, it makes
the equation even a little bit harder.

00:44:21.923 --> 00:44:27.503
But I believe, five years, it'll be
significantly more across the globe.

00:44:27.503 --> 00:44:29.781
A lot of it does depend on fuel prices.

00:44:29.781 --> 00:44:31.230
But I think it's a trend and

00:44:31.230 --> 00:44:35.453
a very important technology that we have
to continue to develop aggressively,

00:44:35.453 --> 00:44:38.679
because it's so important from
an environmental perspective.

00:44:42.392 --> 00:44:45.437
&gt;&gt; Hi Mary, Jeff Howe, MBA one.

00:44:45.437 --> 00:44:49.952
You have a particularly vocal and
public investor in your company right now,

00:44:49.952 --> 00:44:54.830
who's indicated he's not 100% in
agreement with everything GM is deciding.

00:44:54.830 --> 00:44:58.720
I was just curious to hear from you how
you think about managing that dialogue?

00:44:58.720 --> 00:45:02.319
And also how you think about shareholders
in the context of all stakeholders in GM?

00:45:02.319 --> 00:45:07.120
&gt;&gt; Well, shareholders are our owners,
so they're vitally important.

00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:10.536
And that's why we spent so
much time on this particular issue,

00:45:10.536 --> 00:45:12.862
to look to see hey is
there a good idea here?

00:45:12.862 --> 00:45:19.160
Because our job is to do the best,
responsibly, for our share holders.

00:45:19.160 --> 00:45:21.250
And as we evaluated this issue,

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:23.500
we didn't think it was in the best
interest of our share holders.

00:45:23.500 --> 00:45:27.611
And so
I talk regularly to multiple shareholders,

00:45:27.611 --> 00:45:33.170
just two days ago I was in Boston
meeting with several shareholders.

00:45:35.000 --> 00:45:36.780
Spent the day, and
I do that quite regularly.

00:45:36.780 --> 00:45:39.360
So I think it's very important
to listen to your shareholders,

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:40.903
to understand their points of views.

00:45:40.903 --> 00:45:45.115
But at the same time then we have
to take all that information and

00:45:45.115 --> 00:45:47.501
integrate it and then understand,

00:45:47.501 --> 00:45:51.968
because we're at the company day in and
day out, and understand.

00:45:51.968 --> 00:45:55.870
Maybe at a level deeper because
we spend 24/7 on it and so

00:45:55.870 --> 00:45:59.158
we've gotta look at what's
the right decision.

00:45:59.158 --> 00:46:02.147
Let's take all that input now and
let's understand what we're doing.

00:46:02.147 --> 00:46:04.474
Is there a way we can
improve our strategy?

00:46:04.474 --> 00:46:07.667
Is there a different capital
allocation decision we can make?

00:46:07.667 --> 00:46:09.356
We've always gotta stay open to that,

00:46:09.356 --> 00:46:12.280
because not every good idea is going to
necessarily come from the company.

00:46:12.280 --> 00:46:15.334
So that's the way I hugely
value my shareholders,

00:46:15.334 --> 00:46:19.815
and feel it's vitally important to
listen to them on a very regular basis.

00:46:23.206 --> 00:46:26.235
&gt;&gt; Hi, Shan you, class of 07.

00:46:26.235 --> 00:46:27.789
And also a GM shareholder.

00:46:27.789 --> 00:46:28.356
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:46:28.356 --> 00:46:32.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I have a question about automated cars.

00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:36.395
Assuming that the future includes
automated cars talking to each other

00:46:36.395 --> 00:46:40.679
through a protocol, my question is how
do you think we're going to get there?

00:46:40.679 --> 00:46:44.808
Do you think the market's going to pick
a winner among OEMs or tech companies,

00:46:44.808 --> 00:46:46.493
traditional tech companies?

00:46:46.493 --> 00:46:50.648
Or do you think government's going to
set a standard or play a role?

00:46:50.648 --> 00:46:55.344
&gt;&gt; Well I think if you're going to have
the vehicle to vehicle communications

00:46:55.344 --> 00:46:59.380
which we actually have a vehicle
that has a capability on it now.

00:46:59.380 --> 00:47:01.867
It's pretty limited
because it's one model.

00:47:01.867 --> 00:47:04.268
I think the government has to play a role.

00:47:04.268 --> 00:47:10.170
And there's already a standard set and
a spectrum dedicated to it.

00:47:10.170 --> 00:47:12.336
But I think what's happening because,

00:47:12.336 --> 00:47:15.468
think about it,
every car has gotta have the technology.

00:47:15.468 --> 00:47:20.392
The car park right now in
the United States is 240 million vehicles.

00:47:20.392 --> 00:47:25.715
And at a rate of a pretty strong market
now of 17.5 million vehicles a year,

00:47:25.715 --> 00:47:29.280
it's a long journey to have
fully v to v capability.

00:47:30.520 --> 00:47:33.189
Although there's probably
some things you can do, but

00:47:33.189 --> 00:47:36.218
then of adding the technology
to other vehicles, certainly.

00:47:36.218 --> 00:47:40.465
So I think that's a part as we go,
and it's v to v, v to i,

00:47:40.465 --> 00:47:42.693
v to infrastructure as well.

00:47:42.693 --> 00:47:47.340
But I think what's happening
in autonomous vehicles now

00:47:47.340 --> 00:47:50.256
because that's a longer journey.

00:47:50.256 --> 00:47:54.335
Really to do an autonomous vehicle, to
control it, you need to know three things.

00:47:54.335 --> 00:47:56.119
Precisely where am I?

00:47:56.119 --> 00:47:59.486
Precisely what's happening around me?

00:47:59.486 --> 00:48:03.085
And therefore, third,
what path do I take safely?

00:48:03.085 --> 00:48:05.939
And we're doing that
with other technologies.

00:48:05.939 --> 00:48:10.483
But clearly the V2V and the V2I
technology, I think, down the road,

00:48:10.483 --> 00:48:15.481
has it to take it to even another level
of not only safety, but efficiency, but

00:48:15.481 --> 00:48:16.724
we're a ways off.

00:48:19.375 --> 00:48:20.785
Hi
&gt;&gt; My name is Catherine Morgan.

00:48:20.785 --> 00:48:22.122
I'm in MBA 1.

00:48:22.122 --> 00:48:25.295
When you guys were talking about
the roles that cars have played in

00:48:25.295 --> 00:48:28.418
American culture really resonated
with me because I'm from Texas.

00:48:28.418 --> 00:48:32.751
And so I was wondering if you can comment
a little bit about how you see the role

00:48:32.751 --> 00:48:33.360
of cars and

00:48:33.360 --> 00:48:37.582
maybe more specifically GM playing
in the next generation of Americana.

00:48:37.582 --> 00:48:40.888
&gt;&gt; Well, again,
as I started the conversation,

00:48:40.888 --> 00:48:45.847
I think it's our job to not say here
customers the solution for you, but

00:48:45.847 --> 00:48:50.780
to listen to the customer and
say what does technology enable us to do?

00:48:50.780 --> 00:48:54.622
How our city's changing,
how our transportation changing and

00:48:54.622 --> 00:48:59.975
then provide the most efficient best
solution from a customer's perspective.

00:48:59.975 --> 00:49:03.395
So, I think one of the key things we
need to do is really understand what

00:49:03.395 --> 00:49:08.415
the technology's capable of doing,
not only and a wholly world one

00:49:08.415 --> 00:49:11.705
of the time this vehicles of what do you
do when the vehicle because you solve and

00:49:11.705 --> 00:49:12.925
you haven't you know at least for

00:49:12.925 --> 00:49:16.590
period time the physical time of
getting from point a to point b.

00:49:16.590 --> 00:49:18.380
How do we make that more efficient?

00:49:18.380 --> 00:49:21.610
How do we make the environment,
what you're able to do?

00:49:21.610 --> 00:49:26.461
I mean that's why it's so exciting right
now to be in the auto business, or

00:49:26.461 --> 00:49:29.926
the transportation business
because it's really,

00:49:29.926 --> 00:49:34.084
we have to be customer driven in
how we deploy the technology most

00:49:34.084 --> 00:49:36.865
efficiently because I
think that's who wins.

00:49:39.816 --> 00:49:40.520
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon.

00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:44.500
Sort of saying, MSX program,
when you got into GM,

00:49:44.500 --> 00:49:49.758
the way I looked at it, it was starting
up a hundred year old company.

00:49:49.758 --> 00:49:53.393
What were your biggest challenges
when you get into that role and

00:49:53.393 --> 00:49:57.312
you had to like jump start something
that's been around for so long.

00:49:57.312 --> 00:49:58.497
&gt;&gt; Are you talking about
when I became the CEO?

00:49:58.497 --> 00:49:59.137
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:49:59.137 --> 00:50:00.000
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:03.875
I had been part of the leadership,
the senior leadership team for

00:50:03.875 --> 00:50:07.482
a handful of years before I had
the opportunity to be the CEO.

00:50:07.482 --> 00:50:13.257
So we were already working on transforming
the culture, simplifying the company,

00:50:13.257 --> 00:50:18.803
eliminating bureaucracy, making sure
people were empowered to do their best.

00:50:18.803 --> 00:50:21.084
So I'd been kind of been on that journey.

00:50:21.084 --> 00:50:23.092
So a lot of it was continuing and

00:50:23.092 --> 00:50:27.262
accelerating the work that we
were doing to kind of, I'll say,

00:50:27.262 --> 00:50:31.527
push the reset the button after
the bankruptcy with the company.

00:50:31.527 --> 00:50:35.898
And a lot of it really gets to behaviors.

00:50:35.898 --> 00:50:39.885
So if you subscribe that most
people come to work everyday and

00:50:39.885 --> 00:50:43.652
they want to do a good job,
what's getting in their way?

00:50:43.652 --> 00:50:46.912
Do we have an environment and
a collaborative environment and

00:50:46.912 --> 00:50:49.932
the tools that are necessary so
you can do your best work?

00:50:49.932 --> 00:50:55.370
Or is it painful to get
the most simple tasks done.

00:50:55.370 --> 00:50:59.160
And so, a lot of it is just continuing
to make sure that processes and

00:50:59.160 --> 00:51:01.860
systems were efficient and
we're giving people the right tools and

00:51:01.860 --> 00:51:04.060
we're giving them the right training and
then empowering them.

00:51:04.060 --> 00:51:07.277
And that's the journey we've been on,
I would say,

00:51:07.277 --> 00:51:11.011
since even before I had the role and
continue to do that today.

00:51:11.011 --> 00:51:14.947
We spend a lot of time working
on what are the right behaviors,

00:51:14.947 --> 00:51:19.188
what are the company's values,
and how do we really infuse that,

00:51:19.188 --> 00:51:22.520
because then it gets to that
capturing the hearts and

00:51:22.520 --> 00:51:26.635
minds of everybody that are going
to deliver superior results.

00:51:26.635 --> 00:51:31.195
Unfortunately that is all we have time for
so please join me and thank you very much.

00:51:31.195 --> 00:51:32.483
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you all, great questions.

00:51:32.483 --> 00:51:35.813
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:35.813 --> 00:51:39.770
[MUSIC]

